-- title:
--   Drinfeld bases lifting the origin as roots of g, naturally
-- statement:
--   Fixed throughout are a prime $q$, natural numbers $\ell_g$ and $M'$ with $\ell_g$ prime, $\ell_g \equiv 11 \pmod{12}$ and $M' \neq 0$, and a commutative base ring $A_0$.
--
--   *Transport hypotheses over $A_0$.* The hypotheses `hℓ`, `hM`, `hL` require, for every commutative $A_0$-algebra $T$, every Weierstrass curve $W$ over $T$ and every variable change $C$: that [`ModularCurve.IsGamma1Point W ℓg D`](def/ModularCurve_WeierstrassGamma1Pow.html#L10) (the affine equation holds at $(x_P,y_P)$, $\mathrm{pre}\Psi_{\ell_g}$ vanishes at $x_P$, and $(x_Q,y_Q)=(x_P,y_P)$) passes to $C \bullet W$ and `D.variableChange C`; that [`ModularCurve.IsGamma0PowAt W p k h`](def/ModularCurve_WeierstrassGamma0Pow.html#L55) passes to $C \bullet W$ and [`ModularCurve.kernelVariableChangeDeg C (gamma0PowDeg p k) h`](def/ModularCurve_WeierstrassLevelComponents.html#L104); and that a divisibility $h \mid$ [`ModularCurve.inLineMulPoly W ℓg n x`](def/ModularCurve_WeierstrassH1Pow.html#L18) yields `kernelVariableChangeDeg C d h` $\mid$ `inLineMulPoly (C • W) ℓg n` $\bigl(u^{-2}(x-r)\bigr)$ for the data $u,r$ of $C$. The hypotheses `hℓ'`, `hM'`, `hL'` are the same three statements with $A_0$ replaced by the ring $W_0$ introduced below.
--
--   *Group-law data.* A family of relative group laws $\mathcal G$ on the projective models of elliptic Weierstrass curves over $A_0$-algebras, assumed chord–tangent (`h𝒢`: each $\mathcal G\,T\,W\,h_\Delta$ admits a points-evaluation) and origin-identifying (`h𝒢O`: each admits an origin-chart section of the unit with vanishing $x/y$ and $z/y$), together with a level transport $\mathcal T$ for $\mathcal G$ at level $q$ that is a section transport (`h𝒯`). The restriction of scalars of $\mathcal G$ to $W_0$ is likewise assumed chord–tangent and origin-identifying (`h𝒢r`, `h𝒢Or`).
--
--   *The Drinfeld moduli package.* $P_0$ is a representing package (a ring $B_0$ with a universal point, universal for $A_0$-algebra homomorphisms) for the moduli datum attached to `rigidDataH1Pow A₀ ℓg M' q hℓ hM hL 𝒢 𝒯`, i.e. for curves with unit discriminant carrying a family of $\Gamma_0(p^{v_p(M')})$-kernel polynomials indexed by the prime factors of $M'$, a $\Gamma_1(\ell_g)$-point, a Drinfeld $\Gamma(q)$-pair for $\mathcal G$ transported by $\mathcal T$, and the link condition `IsGamma1Link`, all modulo variable change.
--
--   *The local ring $R$ and the residue field.* $R$ is a complete Noetherian local $A_0$-algebra with $\iota : B_0 \to R$ an $A_0$-algebra map; $k$ is a field of characteristic $q$ in which $\ell_g$ and $M'$ are nonzero; $\mathrm{res}_R : R \to k$ is surjective with kernel the maximal ideal. $W_0$ is a complete discrete valuation domain with maximal ideal generated by $q$ (`hW₀`), equipped with a surjection $\mathrm{res}_0 : W_0 \to k$ with kernel the maximal ideal, and with algebra and scalar-tower structures making $\mathrm{res}_R$ restrict to $\mathrm{res}_0$ (`hresR₀`). The hypothesis `hfac` states the universal property of $R$: for every Artinian local $T$ that is simultaneously a $W_0$- and $A_0$-algebra compatibly, every surjection $\mathrm{res}_T : T \to k$ with kernel the maximal ideal restricting to $\mathrm{res}_0$, and every $A_0$-algebra map $\varphi : B_0 \to T$ with $\mathrm{res}_T \circ \varphi = \mathrm{res}_R \circ \iota$, there is a unique $W_0$-algebra map $\Phi : R \to T$ lifting $\mathrm{res}_R$ and satisfying $\Phi \circ \iota = \varphi$.
--
--   *The étale datum.* $P_{\mathrm{et}}$ is a representing package over $W_0$ for the moduli datum of the rigid data obtained from the level component given by the product of `gamma0PowComponent W₀ M' hM'`, `gamma1Component W₀ ℓg hℓ'` and the trivial component, restricted by the condition `IsGamma1Link W ℓg M' x.1 x.2.1` (so no $\Gamma(q)$-structure is carried here); $x_{\mathrm{et}}$ is a raw point of that component over $B_{\mathrm{et}} := P_{\mathrm{et}}.B_0$ — a curve with unit discriminant together with level data satisfying the level conditions — and `hxet` says its class is the universal point of $P_{\mathrm{et}}$. Further, $C_Q$ is a commutative ring that is a $B_{\mathrm{et}}$- and $W_0$-algebra compatibly, and $Q_u$ is a section of the projective model of $x_{\mathrm{et}}.\mathrm{curve}$ over $\operatorname{Spec}(B_{\mathrm{et}} \to C_Q)$, which is $q$-torsion for the restricted group law (`hQu`: its $q$-fold sum is the unit section). The hypothesis `hrep` states that $C_Q$ represents the $q$-torsion: for every $B_{\mathrm{et}}$-algebra $T$ and every $T$-point $Q$ of the projective model over $\operatorname{Spec} T \to \operatorname{Spec} B_{\mathrm{et}}$, the point $Q$ is $q$-torsion if and only if there is a unique $B_{\mathrm{et}}$-algebra map $\chi : C_Q \to T$ with $\operatorname{Spec}(\chi)$ followed by $Q_u$ equal to $Q$.
--
--   *The torsion-lift local ring $S$ and the Igusa factorisation.* $S$ is a complete Noetherian local $W_0$-algebra with a $W_0$-algebra map $\iota_S : C_Q \to S$ and a surjection $\mathrm{res}_S : S \to k$ with kernel the maximal ideal restricting to $\mathrm{res}_0$; `hfacS` is the corresponding universal property of $S$ among Artinian local $W_0$-algebras with residue map to $k$ over $\mathrm{res}_0$ and $W_0$-algebra maps out of $C_Q$. $F_S$ is a one-parameter formal group over $S$ whose power series is the fixed formal group law of $x_{\mathrm{et}}.\mathrm{curve}$ base-changed along $\iota_S \circ (B_{\mathrm{et}} \to C_Q)$ (`hFS`). Finally $g \in S[X]$ is monic of degree $q-1$ with all coefficients below degree $q-1$ in the maximal ideal (`hgcoeff`) and constant coefficient $q$ times a unit (`hg0`), $v$ is a unit power series, and `hfacq` gives the factorisation $[q]_{F_S} = X \cdot g \cdot v$ of the $q$-series of $F_S$; in addition `AdjoinRoot g` is an integral domain (`hdom`) and $q \neq 0$ in $S$ (`hqS`).
--
--   *Residual data over $k$.* Algebra structures make $k$ an $A_0$-, $W_0$- and $B_{\mathrm{et}}$-algebra, with the $W_0$-structure given by $\mathrm{res}_0$ (`hk₀`). There are: an $A_0$-algebra map $\rho : B_0 \to k$ with $\rho = \mathrm{res}_R \circ \iota$; a $W_0$-algebra map $\psi_{\mathrm{et}} : B_{\mathrm{et}} \to k$ with $\psi_{\mathrm{et}}(b) = \mathrm{res}_S(\iota_S(b))$ for all $b$, which also gives the $B_{\mathrm{et}}$-algebra structure of $k$ (`hψalg`); the discriminant of the base change of $x_{\mathrm{et}}.\mathrm{curve}$ along $\psi_{\mathrm{et}}$ is a unit (`hΔk`); and a section $Q_k$ of that curve over $k$. The hypothesis `hQk_pin` provides a graded ring homomorphism $\phi$ between the Proj gradings of the two curves, with the irrelevant ideal of the target contained in the image of that of the source, which is a coefficient homomorphism for $\psi_{\mathrm{et}}$ and for which $Q_k$ followed by $\operatorname{Proj}(\phi)$ equals $\operatorname{Spec}(\mathrm{res}_S \circ \iota_S)$ followed by $Q_u$; `hQk` says $Q_k$ is not the unit section. The reduced level data satisfy the level conditions: `hhk` the $\Gamma_0(p^{v_p(M')})$-condition for each prime factor $p$ of $M'$, `hDk` the $\Gamma_1(\ell_g)$-point condition, `hLk` the link condition, and `hyk` says the raw pair (reduced curve, unit section, $Q_k$) is of level $q$ for $\mathcal G$, i.e. its curve is the reduced curve and its two sections form a Drinfeld basis (equality of basis divisor and $q$-torsion ideal). Lastly `hρyk` identifies the image of the universal point of $P_0$ under $\rho$ with the class of the raw point assembled from the reduced curve, `hΔk`, the reduced kernel polynomials, the reduced $\Gamma_1(\ell_g)$-point, the pair (reduced curve, unit section, $Q_k$), and the proofs `hhk`, `hDk`, `hyk`, `hLk`.
--
--   *Conclusion.* There exists a family $\gamma$ which assigns to every datum consisting of an Artinian local $W_0$-algebra $T$, a surjection $\mathrm{res}_T : T \to k$ with kernel the maximal ideal of $T$ and restricting to $\mathrm{res}_0$ on $W_0$, a $W_0$-algebra map $\Phi : S \to T$, a $W_0$-algebra map $\psi : B_{\mathrm{et}} \to T$ with $\psi(b) = \Phi(\iota_S(b))$ for all $b \in B_{\mathrm{et}}$, and a morphism $Q_m : \operatorname{Spec} T \to \operatorname{Proj}$ of the projective model of $x_{\mathrm{et}}.\mathrm{curve}$ equal to $\operatorname{Spec}(\Phi \circ \iota_S)$ followed by $Q_u$, a bijection
--   $$\bigl\{\, P_m : \operatorname{Spec} T \to \operatorname{Proj} \;\bigm|\; (\ast) \,\bigr\} \;\simeq\; \{\, \pi \in T \mid (g^{\Phi})(\pi) = 0 \,\},$$
--   where $g^{\Phi}$ is the image of $g$ under $\Phi$ and $(\ast)$ is the conjunction of two conditions: first, that $P_m$ and $Q_m$ both lie over $\operatorname{Spec}(\psi)$ via the structural morphism of the projective model and that, as sections over $\operatorname{Spec}(\psi)$, the pair $(P_m, Q_m)$ satisfies `IsDrinfeldBasisOver` at level $q$ for the restricted group law of $x_{\mathrm{et}}.\mathrm{curve}$ over $B_{\mathrm{et}}$ — that is, the relative basis divisor of the pair equals the relative $q$-torsion ideal over $\operatorname{Spec}(\psi)$; and second, that $\operatorname{Spec}(\mathrm{res}_T)$ followed by $P_m$ equals the unit section of that group law over $\operatorname{Spec}(\psi_{\mathrm{et}})$, i.e. $P_m$ reduces to the origin.
--
--   The second conjunct of the conclusion is the naturality of $\gamma$: for all Artinian local $W_0$-algebras $T$ and $T'$ with residue maps $\mathrm{res}_T$, $\mathrm{res}_{T'}$ as above, every $W_0$-algebra map $f : T \to T'$ with $\mathrm{res}_{T'} \circ f = \mathrm{res}_T$, every $\Phi : S \to T$, every $\psi$ and $Q_m$ as above for $\Phi$, and the corresponding $\psi'$ and $Q_m'$ for $f \circ \Phi$, and for all members $P$ and $P'$ of the two subtypes (over $T$ with $\psi, Q_m$, and over $T'$ with $\psi', Q_m'$ respectively): if $P' = \operatorname{Spec}(f)$ followed by $P$, then the root attached to $P'$ by $\gamma$ at $T'$ with the data $f \circ \Phi, \psi', Q_m'$ equals $f$ applied to the root attached to $P$ by $\gamma$ at $T$.
--
--   This is the pointwise description of the Drinfeld $\Gamma(q)$-basis locus above a fixed $q$-torsion point on the universal curve with $\Gamma_0(M') \cap \Gamma_1(\ell_g)$-structure: over Artinian local $W_0$-algebras, the bases reducing to the origin are parametrised, functorially, by the roots of the Igusa polynomial $g$ coming from the factorisation $[q] = X \cdot g \cdot v$ of the $q$-series of the formal group. It is used by [`ModularCurve.LevelModuliPackageAbs.exists_algHom_equiv_subtype_eval_map_eq_zero_natural_of_factorsThrough_of_nthSeries_eq_X_mul_mul_of_isDomain_adjoinRoot_rigidDataH1Pow`](thm.html#ModularCurve.LevelModuliPackageAbs.exists_algHom_equiv_subtype_eval_map_eq_zero_natural_of_factorsThrough_of_nthSeries_eq_X_mul_mul_of_isDomain_adjoinRoot_rigidDataH1Pow), which recasts this family of bijections as a statement about algebra homomorphisms out of the root ring.
